The lesson to draw is to be generally sceptical about planning, especially if it is merely focused on the outcome, not on the actual work
and the steps required to archive a goal. **While it doesn't help to imagine oneself the great author of a successful and timerly finished
paper, it does make a difference if we have a realistc idea about what needs to be done to get there in our minds. We know from sports that 
it doesn't help when athletes imagine themselves as winners of a race, but it makes a big difference if they imagine all the training that is
necessary to be able to win. Haveing a more realistic idea in mind not only helps them to perform better, it also boosts their motivation
(Singles et al. 2001). We know today that this is not only true for athletes, but for any work that needs effort and endurance (Pham and Taylor,
1999). 
